{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 2, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "The first thing James noticed was the unnatural brightness of the room—sunlight poured through windows that shouldn’t exist, casting sharp, pixelated shadows across the wooden floor. His hands, when he lifted them, were too smooth, their movements too precise, like a marionette’s. Panic clawed at his throat as he stumbled to the mirror, only to find his reflection flickering—half his face a smooth, uncanny valley void, the other half his own, terrified eyes. The air smelled faintly of electricity, and the hum of unseen machines buzzed in his ears. He pinched his arm hard enough to bruise, but the pain felt distant, like it belonged to someone else. His phone was dead, its screen cracked in a perfect geometric pattern, and when he tried to speak, his voice came out as a glitchy, distorted whisper.\n\n***\n\nJames bolted for the door, his bare feet slapping against the floorboards that now seemed to ripple under his weight. The handle wouldn’t turn—locked, he realized, or perhaps *scripted* to stay shut. Desperate, he pressed his palm against the wood, and the door dissolved into a swirl of code, revealing a street he didn’t recognize: blocky buildings stretched into a horizon that vanished into static. A few NPCs—people with stiff, repetitive movements—walked past him, their faces blank, their dialogue looping in a language he somehow understood. A child pointed at him and giggled before repeating, “Invalid user. Invalid user.” James’s heart hammered as he spotted a glowing red icon in his peripheral vision—a quest marker, pulsing like a heartbeat. His hands trembled as he raised them again, fingers twitching over an inventory screen that hadn’t been there a second ago. He was trapped. Not just in this world, but in someone else’s game. And if he didn’t figure out the rules fast, he’d be stuck here forever.", "logprobs": [{"token": "The", "logp